# Maze 3D for AerOS: a first-person maze, drawn the way early 3D games were, with ray casting
# Run it with:
#     maze3d
#
# Controls:
#     Up / W, Down / S     - walk forwards and backwards
#     Left / Right         - turn
#     Mouse                - look around (in QEMU, click the window first: Ctrl+Alt+G lets go)
#     A / D                - step sideways
#     Space / mouse click  - shine your light: a ghost it hits straight ahead goes away for a while
#     M                    - show or hide the map
#     Escape               - back to the menu (and on the menu, quit)
#
# Pick up every crystal, then find the gold door. Ghosts drift about the maze, and chase you
# once they are close (their eyes glow red). One touching you costs a life: lose them all and
# the game is over.
#
# The menu at the start chooses the maze: the classic one drawn below, or a new one made up
# for each game in three bigger sizes, how many crystals, ghosts and lives there are, and
# whether there is music and sound (with no sound card, the important sounds are beeps).
#
# How it works, for anyone who wants to build on it:
#
#   The world is a flat grid of squares (MAP, below, or a made-up maze). Each square is
#   either empty floor or a wall. Nothing is really 3D: for every column of the screen, a ray is sent out from the
#   player across the grid until it hits a wall. The further away the wall is, the shorter its
#   column is drawn. Do that for every column, left to right, and the walls look solid.
#
#   The crystals are "sprites": flat shapes that always face you. Each one is placed on the
#   screen with a little maths, and is drawn only in the columns where it is nearer than the
#   wall that ray hit, so walls hide them properly.
#
#   The ghosts are sprites as well, drawn strip by strip in the same way. They move from the
#   middle of one square to the middle of the next, so they never go through a wall. To find
#   their way to you they use a "step map": for every open square, how many steps it is from
#   your square, counted outwards from you (a "breadth first search"), and worked out again
#   each time you move into a new square. A chasing ghost always steps to a square one step
#   nearer you; one that is too far away to chase just wanders, never turning back unless it
#   is in a dead end.
#
#   The bigger mazes are made up by a "recursive backtracker": start with every square a wall,
#   dig out a square, then keep digging to a random neighbour two squares away that is still
#   solid (knocking out the wall in between), and step back along the way you came whenever
#   there is nowhere new to dig. That makes a maze with exactly one way between any two
#   squares; knocking out a few more walls then adds some loops, so you can get round a ghost.
#   The door goes on the outside wall, next to the edge square furthest from the start.
#
#   The map in the corner is a tiny picture (a BMP, one pixel a square) made once for each
#   maze and drawn bigger with image(), which is much quicker than drawing every wall of a big
#   maze as a rectangle on every frame.
#
#   The sounds and music are WAV files in the game's folder (/apps/maze3d/), made from plain
#   waves by tools/make_maze3d_sounds.py in the repository. Each is loaded once with sload(),
#   and splay() plays it while the game carries on: AerOS mixes up to 8 at once, so the music
#   (looped, and quiet) goes on under the footsteps, the crystals and the ghosts.
#
#   Looking up and down is a trick too: rays can only go across the grid, so instead the whole
#   picture is drawn higher or lower on the screen. The horizon (where the walls' middles line up)
#   moves down when you look up, which shows more ceiling, and up when you look down. It isn't
#   true perspective, but it looks right for small angles, and some early 3D games did just this.
#
#   Changing it:
#     - Draw your own maze in MAP: '#', 'B' and 'G' are walls, 'o' is a crystal, 'P' is where
#       you start, 'E' is the door and 'X' is where a ghost starts. Keep a wall all the way
#       round the edge.
#       The menu's crystal and ghost numbers still apply: spare ones are left out (the last
#       ones in the map first), and missing ones are put in at random.
#     - The ghosts' speed, how close they must be to chase you, and how long your light keeps
#       them away are all in the settings below, and so are the menu's sizes and numbers.
#     - New wall colours go in WALL_COLOURS, with a letter for them in read_map().
#     - STRIP_WIDTH trades detail for speed: wider strips mean fewer rays and more frames a second.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The maze
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

let MAP = [
    "################",
    "#P...#.....o..X#",
    "#.##.#.###.##..#",
    "#.#o.......#...#",
    "#.#.BBB.##.#.#.#",
    "#...B..X..o#.#.#",
    "###.B.GGG..#.#.#",
    "#...........#..#",
    "#.###.#.GG.##.##",
    "#.#o..#..G.X...#",
    "#.#.###.....##.#",
    "#...#....BB.#..#",
    "#.#.#.##..B.#o.#",
    "#.#X..#.......##",
    "#...#...#.....E#",
    "################"
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Settings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

let MOVE_SPEED = 2.6        # squares a second
let TURN_SPEED = 2.2        # radians a second (a whole turn is 2 * PI, about 6.3)
let RADIUS = 0.3            # how close you can get to a wall, in squares
let VIEW_WIDTH = 0.66       # how wide the view is: 0.66 gives about 66 degrees
let FOG_DISTANCE = 14.0     # walls fade to their darkest this far away
let STRIP_WIDTH = 8         # pixels per ray (see the notes at the top)
let PICK_UP_DISTANCE = 0.5  # how close you must be to take a crystal
let MOUSE_TURN = 0.004      # radians of turn for each step the mouse moves across
let MOUSE_LOOK = 1.5        # pixels the horizon moves for each step the mouse moves up or down

# The menu's choices. For each maze, the crystals and ghosts there are for each choice.
let MAZE_NAMES = ["CLASSIC", "MEDIUM", "LARGE", "HUGE"]
let MAZE_SIZES = [0, 25, 39, 55]            # squares across and down (0: the classic MAP)
let CRYSTAL_NAMES = ["FEW", "NORMAL", "LOTS"]
let CRYSTALS_FOR = [[3, 5, 10], [6, 12, 24], [10, 20, 40], [16, 32, 60]]
let GHOST_NAMES = ["NONE", "SOME", "MANY"]
let GHOSTS_FOR = [[0, 4, 8], [0, 6, 12], [0, 10, 20], [0, 16, 32]]
let LIVES_FOR = [1, 3, 5]
let SOUND_NAMES = ["ON", "EFFECTS ONLY", "OFF"]
let SOUND_ON = 0            # music and sound effects
let SOUND_OFF = 2           # (1 is the effects without the music)
let SETTING_NAMES = ["MAZE", "CRYSTALS", "GHOSTS", "LIVES", "SOUND"]
let LOOPS = 40              # a made-up maze gets an extra gap in a wall for every this many squares
let SPRITE_DISTANCE = 14.0  # crystals and ghosts further away than this aren't drawn

# Sound. The sounds are WAV files in the game's own folder, made by tools/make_maze3d_sounds.py
let SOUND_FOLDER = "/apps/maze3d/"
let MUSIC_VOLUME = 30       # the music, quietly under the effects (volumes go from 0 to 100)
let STEP_VOLUME = 25        # footsteps, quieter still
let STEP_LENGTH = 0.75      # squares walked between footsteps
let SPOTTED_WAIT = 2500     # milliseconds: the "a ghost has seen you" sound plays at most this often

# The ghosts
let GHOST_SPEED = 1.2       # squares a second while a ghost wanders
let CHASE_SPEED = 1.9       # squares a second while it chases you (you walk faster, so run!)
let CHASE_STEPS = 7         # a ghost chases you when it is this many steps away or nearer
let TOUCH_DISTANCE = 0.75   # how near a ghost must come, in squares, to touch you
let SAFE_TIME = 1500        # milliseconds after a touch before another ghost can touch you
let LIGHT_RANGE = 6.0       # how far your light reaches, in squares
let LIGHT_WAIT = 800        # milliseconds before the light can be shone again
let GHOST_AWAY = 6000       # milliseconds a ghost stays away after the light or a touch
let FAR = 9999              # the step map's "can't get there from here"
# The four ways out of a square, as steps across and down: east, south, west, north. The way
# back from way d is (d + 2) % 4
let STEP_X = [1, 0, -1, 0]
let STEP_Y = [0, 1, 0, -1]

# Wall colours as [red, green, blue], by the number read_map() gives each kind of wall
# (0 is empty floor, so it has no colour)
let WALL_COLOURS = [
    nil,
    [120, 130, 150],        # 1 '#' stone
    [190, 95, 55],          # 2 'B' brick
    [70, 145, 85],          # 3 'G' moss
    [235, 185, 60]          # 4 'E' the door
]
let DOOR = 4

let CEILING = [40, 46, 64]
let FLOOR = [70, 60, 52]
let CRYSTAL = 0x5CE1FF
let CRYSTAL_EDGE = 0xD6F7FF
let TEXT = 0xF2F5F7
let DIM_TEXT = 0x9AA5B1
let GOLD = 0xFFD166
let MAP_BACK = 0x101820
let GHOST = [215, 205, 255]     # a pale lilac, darkened with distance like the walls
let GHOST_EYES = 0x1A1030       # the eyes of a ghost that is wandering
let GHOST_STARE = 0xFF3355      # the eyes of one chasing you: they glow, so fog doesn't dim them
let GHOST_DOT = 0xFF6B6B        # ghosts on the map
let HURT = 0xD8263A             # the screen's edge, for a moment after a ghost touches you
let CAUGHT = 0xFF5A5F           # the words on the screen when the ghosts have won

# What a sprite is, for draw_sprites()
let CRYSTAL_SPRITE = 0
let GHOST_SPRITE = 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Small helpers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

func smaller(a, b)
    if a < b then return a end
    return b
end

func bigger(a, b)
    if a > b then return a end
    return b
end

# A colour from an [r, g, b] list, made darker by `light` (1.0 is full brightness, 0.5 half)
func shade(rgb_list, light)
    return rgb(rgb_list[0] * light, rgb_list[1] * light, rgb_list[2] * light)
end

# Write a line of text in the middle of the screen (each letter is 8 pixels times `size`)
func centre(y, words, colour, size)
    text((gw() - len(words) * 8 * size) / 2, y, words, colour, size)
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sound
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# One sound from the game's folder, or nil if it isn't there (it then just isn't played)
func load_sound(name)
    return sload(SOUND_FOLDER + name + ".wav")
end

# Load every sound once, at the start, so playing one later costs almost nothing
func load_sounds()
    snd_crystal = load_sound("crystal")
    snd_door = load_sound("door")
    snd_light = load_sound("light")
    snd_banish = load_sound("banish")
    snd_hurt = load_sound("hurt")
    snd_step = load_sound("step")
    snd_bump = load_sound("bump")
    snd_spotted = load_sound("spotted")
    snd_won = load_sound("won")
    snd_caught = load_sound("caught")
    snd_menu_move = load_sound("menumove")
    snd_menu_change = load_sound("menuchg")
    snd_start = load_sound("start")
    snd_music_game = load_sound("gamemus")
    snd_music_menu = load_sound("menumus")
    # Is there a sound card? splay() gives -1 without one, so play a sound silently (volume 0)
    # and see
    sound_card = snd_menu_move != nil and splay(snd_menu_move, false, 0) >= 0
end

# Play a sound effect at `volume` (0 to 100), unless the menu says SOUND OFF. With no sound
# card (or no such file), the PC speaker beeps at `hz` instead, if `hz` isn't 0. A beep holds
# the game up while it sounds, so only a few important sounds have one.
func effect(sound, volume, hz)
    if choice[4] == SOUND_OFF then return end
    if sound_card and sound != nil then
        splay(sound, false, volume)
    elif hz > 0 then
        beep(hz)
    end
end

# Play music round and round, quietly under the effects: "menu", "game", or "" for none. Asking
# for the music that is already playing changes nothing, so it carries on smoothly. There is
# music only with SOUND ON (and a sound card).
func music(name)
    if choice[4] != SOUND_ON or not sound_card then name = "" end
    if name == music_name then return end
    if music_voice >= 0 then sstop(music_voice) end
    music_voice = -1
    music_name = name
    let track = nil
    if name == "menu" then track = snd_music_menu end
    if name == "game" then track = snd_music_game end
    if track != nil then music_voice = splay(track, true, MUSIC_VOLUME) end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Making the maze
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Turn MAP's strings into `grid`, a list of rows of numbers (0 floor, 1 to 4 a wall), which is
# quicker to look things up in than letters. Crystals, ghosts and the start become floor, and
# are remembered in `crystals`, `ghost_home`, `start_x` / `start_y` and `door_x` / `door_y`.
func read_map()
    grid = []
    crystals = []
    ghost_home = []
    for y = 0 to len(MAP) - 1 do
        let row = []
        let row_text = MAP[y]
        for x = 0 to len(row_text) - 1 do
            let c = row_text[x]
            let kind = 0
            if c == "#" then
                kind = 1
            elif c == "B" then
                kind = 2
            elif c == "G" then
                kind = 3
            elif c == "E" then
                kind = DOOR
                door_x = x
                door_y = y
            elif c == "o" then
                # +0.5 puts the crystal in the middle of its square
                push(crystals, [x + 0.5, y + 0.5])
            elif c == "P" then
                start_x = x + 0.5
                start_y = y + 0.5
            elif c == "X" then
                push(ghost_home, [x + 0.5, y + 0.5])
            end
            push(row, kind)
        end
        push(grid, row)
    end
end

# Make up a new maze `size` squares across and down (an odd number, so that it has walls all
# round), with the start in the top left corner. See the notes at the top for how.
func make_maze(size)
    # Every square starts as a wall. The walls are coloured in patches of 6 x 6 squares, each
    # patch stone, brick or moss (mostly stone), so parts of the maze look different and are
    # easier to remember
    let patch_kinds = [1, 1, 1, 2, 3]
    let patches_across = size / 6 + 1
    let patches = []
    for i = 0 to patches_across * patches_across - 1 do
        push(patches, patch_kinds[random(len(patch_kinds))])
    end
    grid = []
    for y = 0 to size - 1 do
        let row = []
        for x = 0 to size - 1 do
            push(row, patches[(y / 6) * patches_across + x / 6])
        end
        push(grid, row)
    end

    # Dig. The squares where x and y are both odd are the maze's rooms, and the squares between
    # two rooms are the walls that may be knocked out. `trail` is the way back: the last room
    # in it is the one being dug from.
    grid[1][1] = 0
    let trail = [[1, 1]]
    while len(trail) > 0 do
        let x = trail[len(trail) - 1][0]
        let y = trail[len(trail) - 1][1]
        # the ways to a room two squares off that hasn't been dug yet (it is still a wall)
        let ways = []
        for way = 0 to 3 do
            let to_x = x + STEP_X[way] * 2
            let to_y = y + STEP_Y[way] * 2
            if to_x > 0 and to_x < size - 1 and to_y > 0 and to_y < size - 1 and grid[to_y][to_x] != 0 then
                push(ways, way)
            end
        end
        if len(ways) == 0 then
            # nowhere new to dig from here: step back
            pop(trail)
        else
            # dig through the wall to a random one of them, and carry on from there
            let way = ways[random(len(ways))]
            grid[y + STEP_Y[way]][x + STEP_X[way]] = 0
            grid[y + STEP_Y[way] * 2][x + STEP_X[way] * 2] = 0
            push(trail, [x + STEP_X[way] * 2, y + STEP_Y[way] * 2])
        end
    end

    # Knock out some more walls between rooms, to make loops. Such a wall has one odd and one
    # even position (so x + y is odd), and is never on the edge of the maze
    let gaps = size * size / LOOPS
    while gaps > 0 do
        let x = 1 + random(size - 2)
        let y = 1 + random(size - 2)
        if (x + y) % 2 == 1 and grid[y][x] != 0 then
            grid[y][x] = 0
            gaps = gaps - 1
        end
    end

    start_x = 1.5
    start_y = 1.5
    crystals = []
    ghost_home = []
end

# Put the door in the outside wall, next to the square on the edge of the maze that is the
# most steps from the start. `from_start` is the step map from the start (see steps_from).
func place_door(from_start)
    let size = len(grid)
    let far_x = 1
    let far_y = 1
    let most = -1
    for y = 1 to size - 2 do
        for x = 1 to size - 2 do
            let on_edge = x == 1 or y == 1 or x == size - 2 or y == size - 2
            if on_edge and from_start[y][x] < FAR and from_start[y][x] > most then
                most = from_start[y][x]
                far_x = x
                far_y = y
            end
        end
    end
    # the outside wall beside it (in a corner there are two, and either will do)
    door_x = far_x
    door_y = far_y
    if far_x == size - 2 then
        door_x = size - 1
    elif far_y == size - 2 then
        door_y = size - 1
    elif far_x == 1 then
        door_x = 0
    else
        door_y = 0
    end
    grid[door_y][door_x] = DOOR
end

# A step map: for every square, how many steps it is from (from_x, from_y) without going
# through a wall. Walls, and squares more than `limit` steps away, are FAR.
func steps_from(from_x, from_y, limit)
    let counts = []
    for y = 0 to len(grid) - 1 do
        push(counts, list(len(grid[y]), FAR))
    end
    # Start at that square, and spread outwards one step at a time: every square in `waiting`
    # is looked at in the order it was found, so each one is reached by the shortest way
    # first. (The maze has walls all round, so a neighbour is never off the edge)
    counts[from_y][from_x] = 0
    let waiting = [[from_x, from_y]]
    let next = 0
    while next < len(waiting) do
        let x = waiting[next][0]
        let y = waiting[next][1]
        next = next + 1
        if counts[y][x] < limit then
            for way = 0 to 3 do
                let near_x = x + STEP_X[way]
                let near_y = y + STEP_Y[way]
                if grid[near_y][near_x] == 0 and counts[near_y][near_x] == FAR then
                    counts[near_y][near_x] = counts[y][x] + 1
                    push(waiting, [near_x, near_y])
                end
            end
        end
    end
    return counts
end

# Mark the square of every spot (a list of [x, y]) in `used`
func mark_used(used, spots)
    for spot in spots do
        used[floor(spot[1])][floor(spot[0])] = true
    end
end

# Make `spots` (a list of [x, y], like `crystals`) `wanted` long. If there are too many, the
# last ones are left out. If too few, squares are added at random: open floor, at least
# `min_steps` from the start, and not `used` already. (If there aren't enough such squares,
# it stays shorter)
func fit_spots(spots, wanted, min_steps, from_start, used)
    while len(spots) > wanted do
        pop(spots)
    end
    if len(spots) == wanted then return end

    let free = []
    for y = 0 to len(grid) - 1 do
        for x = 0 to len(grid[y]) - 1 do
            if from_start[y][x] >= min_steps and from_start[y][x] < FAR and not used[y][x] then
                push(free, [x, y])
            end
        end
    end
    while len(spots) < wanted and len(free) > 0 do
        let pick = random(len(free))
        let square = free[pick]
        remove(free, pick)
        used[square[1]][square[0]] = true
        # +0.5 puts it in the middle of its square
        push(spots, [square[0] + 0.5, square[1] + 0.5])
    end
end

# Write a number into 4 bytes of `data` from `at`, smallest part first (the way BMP files
# keep their numbers)
func put_number(data, at, number)
    for i = 0 to 3 do
        data[at + i] = number % 256
        number = number / 256
    end
end

# The map's picture (see the notes at the top): a BMP with one pixel for each square, walls in
# their colours on the map's background. A BMP is a 54 byte header (the letters "BM", sizes,
# and how many bits each pixel has), then the rows of pixels, the bottom row first. Each pixel
# is three bytes, blue, green, red, and each row is padded to a multiple of 4 bytes.
func make_map_picture()
    let w = len(grid[0])
    let h = len(grid)
    let row_size = (w * 3 + 3) / 4 * 4
    map_picture = bytes(54 + row_size * h)
    map_picture[0] = ord("B")
    map_picture[1] = ord("M")
    put_number(map_picture, 2, 54 + row_size * h)   # the size of the whole file
    put_number(map_picture, 10, 54)                 # where the pixels start
    put_number(map_picture, 14, 40)                 # the size of the rest of the header
    put_number(map_picture, 18, w)
    put_number(map_picture, 22, h)
    map_picture[26] = 1                             # "planes": always 1
    map_picture[28] = 24                            # bits a pixel
    # (everything else in the header is 0: no compression, and the usual defaults)

    # the background's red, green and blue, from MAP_BACK (0xRRGGBB)
    let back = [MAP_BACK / 65536, MAP_BACK / 256 % 256, MAP_BACK % 256]
    for y = 0 to h - 1 do
        let at = 54 + (h - 1 - y) * row_size
        for x = 0 to w - 1 do
            let colour = back
            if grid[y][x] != 0 then
                let wall = WALL_COLOURS[grid[y][x]]
                colour = [floor(wall[0] * 0.8), floor(wall[1] * 0.8), floor(wall[2] * 0.8)]
            end
            map_picture[at] = colour[2]
            map_picture[at + 1] = colour[1]
            map_picture[at + 2] = colour[0]
            at = at + 3
        end
    end
end

# Make the maze chosen on the menu: `grid`, the start, the door, the crystals, where the
# ghosts start, and the map's picture
func build_maze()
    let size = MAZE_SIZES[choice[0]]
    if size == 0 then
        read_map()
    else
        make_maze(size)
    end

    # How many steps each square is from the start, so the door can go far away, and nothing
    # is put right beside you
    let from_start = steps_from(floor(start_x), floor(start_y), FAR)
    if size != 0 then place_door(from_start) end

    # Which squares have something on them, so two things never share a square
    let used = []
    for y = 0 to len(grid) - 1 do
        push(used, list(len(grid[y]), false))
    end
    used[floor(start_y)][floor(start_x)] = true
    mark_used(used, crystals)
    mark_used(used, ghost_home)
    fit_spots(crystals, CRYSTALS_FOR[choice[0]][choice[1]], 3, from_start, used)
    # ghosts start well out of reach, so none chases you straight away
    fit_spots(ghost_home, GHOSTS_FOR[choice[0]][choice[2]], CHASE_STEPS + 4, from_start, used)

    make_map_picture()
end

# Is the square under this point (in squares, decimals allowed) a wall?
func is_wall(x, y)
    return grid[floor(y)][floor(x)] != 0
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The player
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The player stands at (pos_x, pos_y), facing `angle` (0 is east, PI / 2 is south: on the
# screen y grows downwards). From the angle come two arrows the drawing needs:
#   dir   - one square straight ahead
#   plane - the "camera plane", pointing to the right, VIEW_WIDTH long. A ray for a column of
#           the screen is dir plus some of plane: none of it for the middle column, all of it
#           for the right edge, and minus all of it for the left edge.
func face(new_angle)
    angle = new_angle
    dir_x = cos(angle)
    dir_y = sin(angle)
    plane_x = -dir_y * VIEW_WIDTH
    plane_y = dir_x * VIEW_WIDTH
end

# Move by (dx, dy) squares, one direction at a time, so walking into a wall at an angle slides
# along it instead of stopping dead. RADIUS keeps you from standing inside the wall's face.
func move(dx, dy)
    let edge_x = RADIUS
    if dx < 0 then edge_x = -RADIUS end
    if not is_wall(pos_x + dx + edge_x, pos_y) then
        pos_x = pos_x + dx
    end
    let edge_y = RADIUS
    if dy < 0 then edge_y = -RADIUS end
    if not is_wall(pos_x, pos_y + dy + edge_y) then
        pos_y = pos_y + dy
    end
end

# Read the keys and move, `seconds` being how long the last frame took, so you move at the
# same speed however many frames a second the computer manages
func handle_movement(seconds)
    let walk = MOVE_SPEED * seconds
    let turn = TURN_SPEED * seconds
    let was_x = pos_x
    let was_y = pos_y
    let walking = false

    if keydown(KEY_LEFT) then face(angle - turn) end
    if keydown(KEY_RIGHT) then face(angle + turn) end
    if keydown(KEY_UP) or keydown("w") then
        move(dir_x * walk, dir_y * walk)
        walking = true
    end
    if keydown(KEY_DOWN) or keydown("s") then
        move(-dir_x * walk, -dir_y * walk)
        walking = true
    end
    # sideways is along the camera plane, which points right (divided by VIEW_WIDTH to make
    # it one square long, like dir)
    if keydown("d") then
        move(plane_x / VIEW_WIDTH * walk, plane_y / VIEW_WIDTH * walk)
        walking = true
    end
    if keydown("a") then
        move(-plane_x / VIEW_WIDTH * walk, -plane_y / VIEW_WIDTH * walk)
        walking = true
    end
    footsteps(walking, walk, sqrt((pos_x - was_x) * (pos_x - was_x) + (pos_y - was_y) * (pos_y - was_y)))

    # The mouse: moving it across turns, moving it up and down looks up and down. mousemove()
    # gives how far it moved since the last frame, and never stops at the edge of the screen,
    # so you can keep turning. (With no mouse it gives [0, 0], and the keys still work)
    let moved = mousemove()
    if moved[0] != 0 then face(angle + moved[0] * MOUSE_TURN) end
    # Up on the mouse is negative, and looking up moves the horizon down: hence the minus.
    # It stops a third of the screen either way, past which the trick stops looking right
    look = bigger(smaller(look - moved[1] * MOUSE_LOOK, SCREEN_H / 3), -SCREEN_H / 3)
end

# The sounds of walking: a footstep every STEP_LENGTH squares, and a bump on walking into a
# wall. `walking` says if a key to walk is held, `walk` is how far it should have taken you
# this frame, and `moved` how far you really went. Hardly moving at all means a wall is in the
# way (sliding along one at an angle still moves you): that bumps once, and not again until
# you have moved or let go of the keys.
func footsteps(walking, walk, moved)
    stride = stride + moved
    if stride >= STEP_LENGTH then
        stride = stride - STEP_LENGTH
        effect(snd_step, STEP_VOLUME, 0)
    end
    if walking and moved < walk * 0.2 then
        if not against_wall then effect(snd_bump, 60, 0) end
        against_wall = true
    else
        against_wall = false
    end
end

# Where the horizon is on the screen: the middle, moved by looking up or down
func horizon()
    return SCREEN_H / 2 + look
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ghosts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Each ghost's details are kept in lists, one place in each list per ghost:
#   ghost_x / ghost_y       - where it is now
#   ghost_to_x / ghost_to_y - the middle of the square it is heading for
#   ghost_way               - which way it last went (0 to 3, see STEP_X), or -1 at the start
#   ghost_back              - 0 while it is in the maze; while it is away, the time it comes back
#   ghost_chasing           - whether it was chasing you last frame (to hear when it starts)

# Put every ghost back where it started, ready for a new game
func reset_ghosts()
    ghost_x = []
    ghost_y = []
    ghost_to_x = []
    ghost_to_y = []
    ghost_way = []
    ghost_back = []
    ghost_chasing = []
    for i = 0 to len(ghost_home) - 1 do
        push(ghost_x, ghost_home[i][0])
        push(ghost_y, ghost_home[i][1])
        push(ghost_to_x, ghost_home[i][0])
        push(ghost_to_y, ghost_home[i][1])
        push(ghost_way, -1)
        push(ghost_back, 0)
        push(ghost_chasing, false)
    end
    # so the step map is worked out afresh on the first frame
    steps_x = -1
end

# Work out the ghosts' step map: in `steps`, how many steps each square is from the player's
# square (see steps_from). It only changes when the player moves into another square, so most
# frames this does nothing. It only goes as far as a ghost can chase from (squares further
# away are FAR), which keeps it quick however big the maze is.
func work_out_steps()
    let from_x = floor(pos_x)
    let from_y = floor(pos_y)
    if from_x == steps_x and from_y == steps_y then return end
    steps_x = from_x
    steps_y = from_y
    steps = steps_from(from_x, from_y, CHASE_STEPS)
end

# Is ghost number i near enough to be chasing the player?
func is_chasing(i)
    return steps[floor(ghost_y[i])][floor(ghost_x[i])] <= CHASE_STEPS
end

# Ghost number i has reached the middle of a square: choose the next square to go to
func choose_way(i)
    let x = floor(ghost_x[i])
    let y = floor(ghost_y[i])
    let chosen = -1
    if is_chasing(i) then
        # Any way that is one step nearer the player (on the player's own square, stay put)
        for way = 0 to 3 do
            if steps[y + STEP_Y[way]][x + STEP_X[way]] < steps[y][x] then chosen = way end
        end
    else
        # Wander: pick any open way at random, except back the way it came
        let ways = []
        for way = 0 to 3 do
            if grid[y + STEP_Y[way]][x + STEP_X[way]] == 0 and way != (ghost_way[i] + 2) % 4 then
                push(ways, way)
            end
        end
        if len(ways) > 0 then
            chosen = ways[random(len(ways))]
        elif ghost_way[i] >= 0 then
            # a dead end: the only way out is back
            chosen = (ghost_way[i] + 2) % 4
        end
    end
    if chosen >= 0 then
        ghost_way[i] = chosen
        ghost_to_x[i] = x + STEP_X[chosen] + 0.5
        ghost_to_y[i] = y + STEP_Y[chosen] + 0.5
    end
end

# Move every ghost that is in the maze towards the square it is heading for, and bring back
# any whose time away is up
func move_ghosts(seconds, now)
    for i = 0 to len(ghost_home) - 1 do
        if ghost_back[i] != 0 then
            # It comes back where it started, but not while you are standing right there
            let dx = ghost_home[i][0] - pos_x
            let dy = ghost_home[i][1] - pos_y
            if now >= ghost_back[i] and dx * dx + dy * dy > 9 then
                ghost_back[i] = 0
                ghost_x[i] = ghost_home[i][0]
                ghost_y[i] = ghost_home[i][1]
                ghost_to_x[i] = ghost_x[i]
                ghost_to_y[i] = ghost_y[i]
                ghost_way[i] = -1
            end
            continue
        end

        let speed = GHOST_SPEED
        let chasing = is_chasing(i)
        if chasing then speed = CHASE_SPEED end
        # A ghost that has just started to chase you makes itself heard (but not over and
        # over, when several are about)
        if chasing and not ghost_chasing[i] and now - spotted_at > SPOTTED_WAIT then
            effect(snd_spotted, 70, 0)
            spotted_at = now
        end
        ghost_chasing[i] = chasing
        let can_go = speed * seconds
        # A ghost only ever moves straight across or straight up and down, so the distance to
        # where it is heading is just the two gaps added up
        let dx = ghost_to_x[i] - ghost_x[i]
        let dy = ghost_to_y[i] - ghost_y[i]
        let gap = abs(dx) + abs(dy)
        if gap <= can_go then
            ghost_x[i] = ghost_to_x[i]
            ghost_y[i] = ghost_to_y[i]
            choose_way(i)
        else
            ghost_x[i] = ghost_x[i] + dx / gap * can_go
            ghost_y[i] = ghost_y[i] + dy / gap * can_go
        end
    end
end

# Has a ghost touched the player? It costs a life, and that ghost goes away for a while. Gives
# true when that was the last life.
func check_ghosts(now)
    if now < safe_until then return false end
    for i = 0 to len(ghost_home) - 1 do
        if ghost_back[i] == 0 then
            let dx = ghost_x[i] - pos_x
            let dy = ghost_y[i] - pos_y
            if dx * dx + dy * dy < TOUCH_DISTANCE * TOUCH_DISTANCE then
                lives = lives - 1
                ghost_back[i] = now + GHOST_AWAY
                hurt_at = now
                safe_until = now + SAFE_TIME
                effect(snd_hurt, 90, 160)
                return lives <= 0
            end
        end
    end
    return false
end

# Shine the light straight ahead: the nearest ghost in the middle of the view, nearer than the
# wall there and within LIGHT_RANGE, goes away for a while
func shine_light(now)
    if now < light_ready then return end
    light_ready = now + LIGHT_WAIT
    light_at = now

    let hit = -1
    let nearest = LIGHT_RANGE
    for i = 0 to len(ghost_home) - 1 do
        if ghost_back[i] == 0 then
            let view = to_view(ghost_x[i], ghost_y[i])
            let across = view[0]
            let depth = view[1]
            # On the screen (see draw_ghost) a ghost's middle is SCREEN_W / 2 * across / depth
            # from the centre, and it reaches SCREEN_H / depth / 4 either side of that. The
            # light hits it if the centre of the screen is inside it: multiplying both by
            # depth, that is the sum below.
            let in_the_middle = abs(across) * SCREEN_W / 2 < SCREEN_H / 4.0
            if in_the_middle and depth > 0.2 and depth < nearest and depth < depths[RAYS / 2] then
                hit = i
                nearest = depth
            end
        end
    end
    # the light's sound either way, and the ghost's as it goes (without a sound card, one beep:
    # high for a hit, low for a miss)
    if hit >= 0 then
        ghost_back[hit] = now + GHOST_AWAY
        effect(snd_light, 60, 0)
        effect(snd_banish, 85, 1500)
    else
        effect(snd_light, 60, 600)
    end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Drawing the walls (ray casting)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The ceiling and floor: a few bands each, darker towards the horizon, which is far away. The
# ceiling fills the screen above the horizon and the floor below it, so looking up or down
# changes how tall their bands are. The walls are drawn on top.
func draw_backdrop()
    let bands = 8
    let middle = horizon()
    let ceiling_band = middle / bands + 1
    let floor_band = (SCREEN_H - middle) / bands + 1
    for i = 0 to bands - 1 do
        let light = 1.0 - i / (bands * 1.6)
        rect(0, i * ceiling_band, SCREEN_W, ceiling_band, shade(CEILING, light))
        rect(0, SCREEN_H - (i + 1) * floor_band, SCREEN_W, floor_band, shade(FLOOR, light))
    end
end

# Send one ray per strip of the screen and draw the wall it hits. Each ray's distance is kept
# in `depths`, so draw_sprites() knows which walls are in front of a crystal or a ghost.
func draw_walls(now)
    # The door glows once every crystal has been picked up (sin swings from -1 to 1)
    let door_light = 0.75
    # worked out once here rather than for every ray
    let middle = horizon()
    if taken == len(crystals) then
        door_light = 0.85 + sin(now / 150.0) * 0.15
    end

    for column = 0 to RAYS - 1 do
        # Where this column is across the screen: -1 at the left edge, 0 in the middle, 1 at
        # the right. The ray's direction is dir plus that much of the camera plane.
        let across = 2.0 * column / RAYS - 1.0
        let ray_x = dir_x + plane_x * across
        let ray_y = dir_y + plane_y * across

        # The square the ray starts in
        let square_x = floor(pos_x)
        let square_y = floor(pos_y)

        # How far the ray goes to cross one whole square, east-west and north-south. A ray
        # that never moves in a direction would never cross a line that way: a huge number.
        let cross_x = 1.0e30
        if ray_x != 0 then cross_x = abs(1.0 / ray_x) end
        let cross_y = 1.0e30
        if ray_y != 0 then cross_y = abs(1.0 / ray_y) end

        # Which way the ray steps through the grid, and how far it is to the first grid line
        # each way (the player is part way across a square)
        let step_x = 1
        let next_x = (square_x + 1.0 - pos_x) * cross_x
        if ray_x < 0 then
            step_x = -1
            next_x = (pos_x - square_x) * cross_x
        end
        let step_y = 1
        let next_y = (square_y + 1.0 - pos_y) * cross_y
        if ray_y < 0 then
            step_y = -1
            next_y = (pos_y - square_y) * cross_y
        end

        # Walk the ray square by square, always taking whichever grid line comes first, until
        # it is in a wall. (This is the "DDA" step. The maze has walls all round, so every ray
        # hits one.) `side` says which kind of line it crossed last: 0 east-west, 1 north-south
        let wall = 0
        let side = 0
        while wall == 0 do
            if next_x < next_y then
                next_x = next_x + cross_x
                square_x = square_x + step_x
                side = 0
            else
                next_y = next_y + cross_y
                square_y = square_y + step_y
                side = 1
            end
            wall = grid[square_y][square_x]
        end

        # How far away the wall is, measured straight ahead rather than along the ray (which
        # would bend straight walls into curves, the "fish-eye" effect). Taking back the last
        # step gives exactly that.
        let distance = next_x - cross_x
        if side == 1 then distance = next_y - cross_y end
        distance = bigger(distance, 0.05)
        depths[column] = distance

        # A wall one square away fills the screen's height. Twice as far, half as tall.
        let height = SCREEN_H / distance
        # (its middle is on the horizon)
        let top = middle - height / 2

        # Further walls are darker, and north-south faces are a little darker than east-west
        # ones, which is what makes corners easy to see
        let light = bigger(1.0 - distance / FOG_DISTANCE, 0.15)
        if side == 1 then light = light * 0.7 end
        if wall == DOOR then light = light * door_light / 0.75 end

        # Where along the wall's face the ray hit, from 0 to 1 across one square: the point it
        # hit is the player's position plus `distance` times the ray. Strips near either edge
        # of a square are drawn darker, so each block of wall stands out from the next.
        let along = pos_y + distance * ray_y
        if side == 1 then along = pos_x + distance * ray_x end
        along = along - floor(along)
        if along < 0.025 or along > 0.975 then light = light * 0.75 end

        # The wall, then a darker band along its foot where it meets the floor
        let colour = WALL_COLOURS[wall]
        let x = column * STRIP_WIDTH
        rect(x, top, STRIP_WIDTH, height, shade(colour, light))
        rect(x, top + height * 0.93, STRIP_WIDTH, height * 0.07, shade(colour, light * 0.55))
    end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Drawing the crystals (sprites)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where a point in the maze appears from the player's eye: gives [across, depth]. depth is how
# far ahead it is (negative means behind you), and across how far to the side, both in the
# same "straight ahead" measure the walls use. This undoes the dir / plane arrows.
func to_view(x, y)
    let dx = x - pos_x
    let dy = y - pos_y
    let inverse = 1.0 / (plane_x * dir_y - dir_x * plane_y)
    return [inverse * (dir_y * dx - dir_x * dy), inverse * (-plane_y * dx + plane_x * dy)]
end

# Add the sprite at (x, y) in the maze to `visible`, unless it is far away, behind you or off
# to the side. `visible` is kept in order, furthest first (only a few sprites are in view at
# once, so putting each one straight into its place, an "insertion sort", is plenty). Each
# entry is [across, depth, kind, number].
func add_sprite(visible, x, y, kind, number)
    # a quick check first, as a big maze has dozens of sprites: is it near enough to matter?
    let dx = x - pos_x
    let dy = y - pos_y
    if dx * dx + dy * dy > SPRITE_DISTANCE * SPRITE_DISTANCE then return end
    let view = to_view(x, y)
    # across / depth is -1 at the screen's left edge and 1 at its right, and a sprite is about
    # half a square wide, so past 1.5 none of it shows
    if view[1] > 0.2 and abs(view[0]) < view[1] * 1.5 then
        push(view, kind)
        push(view, number)
        let place = 0
        while place < len(visible) and visible[place][1] > view[1] do
            place = place + 1
        end
        insert(visible, place, view)
    end
end

# Draw every crystal still there and every ghost in the maze, furthest first, so nearer ones
# cover the ones behind them
func draw_sprites(now)
    let visible = []
    for i = 0 to len(crystals) - 1 do
        if not picked[i] then add_sprite(visible, crystals[i][0], crystals[i][1], CRYSTAL_SPRITE, i) end
    end
    for i = 0 to len(ghost_home) - 1 do
        if ghost_back[i] == 0 then add_sprite(visible, ghost_x[i], ghost_y[i], GHOST_SPRITE, i) end
    end

    for v = 0 to len(visible) - 1 do
        let sprite = visible[v]
        if sprite[2] == CRYSTAL_SPRITE then
            draw_crystal(sprite[0], sprite[1], sprite[3], now)
        else
            draw_ghost(sprite[0], sprite[1], sprite[3], now)
        end
    end
end

# Draw crystal number `number`, which to_view() put at `across` and `depth`
func draw_crystal(across, depth, number, now)
    # The same sum as the rays, backwards: where on the screen it is, and how big
    let centre_x = SCREEN_W / 2 * (1.0 + across / depth)
    let size = SCREEN_H / depth * 0.35
    # It floats just above the floor and bobs gently (each one a little out of step)
    let bob = sin(now / 300.0 + number) * size * 0.12
    let centre_y = horizon() + SCREEN_H / depth * 0.18 + bob

    # A diamond, drawn one strip at a time: tallest in the middle, nothing at its sides.
    # A strip is only drawn where this crystal is nearer than the wall that ray hit.
    let first = bigger(floor((centre_x - size / 2) / STRIP_WIDTH), 0)
    let last = smaller(floor((centre_x + size / 2) / STRIP_WIDTH), RAYS - 1)
    for column = first to last do
        if depths[column] > depth then
            let middle = column * STRIP_WIDTH + STRIP_WIDTH / 2
            let from_centre = abs(middle - centre_x) / (size / 2)
            let half = size / 2 * (1.0 - from_centre)
            if half > 0 then
                rect(column * STRIP_WIDTH, centre_y - half, STRIP_WIDTH, half * 2, CRYSTAL)
                rect(column * STRIP_WIDTH, centre_y - half, STRIP_WIDTH, half / 3, CRYSTAL_EDGE)
            end
        end
    end
end

# Draw ghost number `number`, which to_view() put at `across` and `depth`: a round head, a
# wavy hem that ripples, and two eyes (red while it chases you)
func draw_ghost(across, depth, number, now)
    let centre_x = SCREEN_W / 2 * (1.0 + across / depth)
    # `tall` is how tall a wall this far away is drawn, so the ghost is sized to match: half a
    # square wide, and two thirds of a wall high
    let tall = SCREEN_H / depth
    let half = tall / 4
    let bob = sin(now / 250.0 + number * 2) * tall * 0.03
    # Its hem hangs a little above the floor (the floor is half a wall below the horizon), and
    # its head is a half circle, whose middle is `head`
    let foot = horizon() + tall * 0.36 + bob
    let head = foot - tall * 0.42

    # Darker further away, like the walls. Strips near its sides are a little darker still, to
    # make it look round.
    let light = bigger(1.0 - depth / FOG_DISTANCE, 0.25)
    let body = shade(GHOST, light)
    let edge = shade(GHOST, light * 0.78)
    let eyes = GHOST_EYES
    if is_chasing(number) then eyes = GHOST_STARE end

    let first = bigger(floor((centre_x - half) / STRIP_WIDTH), 0)
    let last = smaller(floor((centre_x + half) / STRIP_WIDTH), RAYS - 1)
    for column = first to last do
        if depths[column] > depth then
            # where this strip crosses the ghost: -1 at its left side, 0 its middle, 1 its right
            let middle = column * STRIP_WIDTH + STRIP_WIDTH / 2
            let u = (middle - centre_x) / half
            if abs(u) < 1.0 then
                # the top of a circle is sqrt(1 - u * u) above its middle
                let top = head - half * sqrt(1.0 - u * u)
                # the hem goes up and down across the ghost, and the waves move along with time
                let hem = foot - (1.0 + sin(u * 8.0 + now / 120.0)) * tall * 0.03
                let x = column * STRIP_WIDTH
                let colour = body
                if abs(u) > 0.7 then colour = edge end
                rect(x, top, STRIP_WIDTH, hem - top, colour)
                if abs(u) > 0.15 and abs(u) < 0.55 then
                    rect(x, head - tall * 0.01, STRIP_WIDTH, tall * 0.09, eyes)
                end
            end
        end
    end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The map and the words on top
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A small map in the top right corner: the walls, the crystals left, the ghosts, and you as a
# dot with a line showing which way you face
func draw_map()
    # 6 pixels a square, or fewer for a big maze, so the map is never more than 240 across
    let cell = smaller(6, 240 / len(grid[0]))
    let width = len(grid[0]) * cell
    let height = len(grid) * cell
    let left = SCREEN_W - width - 10
    let top = 10
    rect(left - 2, top - 2, width + 4, height + 4, MAP_BACK)
    # the walls: the map's picture, drawn `cell` times bigger
    image(map_picture, left, top, width, height)
    for i = 0 to len(crystals) - 1 do
        if not picked[i] then
            rect(left + crystals[i][0] * cell - 1, top + crystals[i][1] * cell - 1, 3, 3, CRYSTAL)
        end
    end
    for i = 0 to len(ghost_home) - 1 do
        if ghost_back[i] == 0 then
            rect(left + ghost_x[i] * cell - 2, top + ghost_y[i] * cell - 2, 4, 4, GHOST_DOT)
        end
    end
    let me_x = left + pos_x * cell
    let me_y = top + pos_y * cell
    line(me_x, me_y, me_x + dir_x * 8, me_y + dir_y * 8, GOLD)
    rect(me_x - 1, me_y - 1, 3, 3, TEXT)
end

# The crystal count, the time, the lives left, a hint, and the frame rate (handy while
# changing things)
func draw_status(now, fps)
    text(12, 12, "CRYSTALS " + str(taken) + " / " + str(len(crystals)), CRYSTAL, 2)
    text(12, 40, "TIME " + str((now - started) / 1000), TEXT, 2)
    text(12, 68, "LIVES " + str(lives), GHOST_DOT, 2)
    if taken == len(crystals) then
        text(12, 96, "FIND THE GOLD DOOR", GOLD, 2)
    end
    text(12, SCREEN_H - 20, str(fps) + " FPS   SPACE LIGHT   M MAP   ESC QUIT", DIM_TEXT, 1)
end

# A small cross in the middle of the view, where the light shines (dim while it gets ready
# again), the beam for a moment after shining it, and a red edge round the screen for a
# moment after a ghost touches you
func draw_light_and_hurt(now)
    let centre_x = SCREEN_W / 2
    let centre_y = horizon()
    if now - light_at < 120 then
        # the beam: lines fanning in from the bottom of the screen to the middle
        for i = 0 to 8 do
            line(centre_x - 64 + i * 16, SCREEN_H, centre_x, centre_y, CRYSTAL_EDGE)
        end
        rect(centre_x - 6, centre_y - 6, 12, 12, CRYSTAL_EDGE)
    end
    let colour = CRYSTAL
    if now < light_ready then colour = DIM_TEXT end
    rect(centre_x - 10, centre_y - 1, 6, 2, colour)
    rect(centre_x + 4, centre_y - 1, 6, 2, colour)
    rect(centre_x - 1, centre_y - 10, 2, 6, colour)
    rect(centre_x - 1, centre_y + 4, 2, 6, colour)

    if now - hurt_at < 400 then draw_hurt_edge() end
end

# A red edge all round the screen
func draw_hurt_edge()
    let thick = 14
    rect(0, 0, SCREEN_W, thick, HURT)
    rect(0, SCREEN_H - thick, SCREEN_W, thick, HURT)
    rect(0, 0, thick, SCREEN_H, HURT)
    rect(SCREEN_W - thick, 0, thick, SCREEN_H, HURT)
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Crystals and the door
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Take any crystal close enough, and see if the door has been reached. Gives true when the
# maze is finished.
func check_pickups()
    for i = 0 to len(crystals) - 1 do
        if not picked[i] then
            let dx = crystals[i][0] - pos_x
            let dy = crystals[i][1] - pos_y
            if dx * dx + dy * dy < PICK_UP_DISTANCE * PICK_UP_DISTANCE then
                picked[i] = true
                taken = taken + 1
                effect(snd_crystal, 80, 1200)
                # the last one: the door can open now
                if taken == len(crystals) then effect(snd_door, 80, 0) end
            end
        end
    end
    # The door is a wall, so you can only get next to it: within about a square of its middle
    if taken == len(crystals) then
        let dx = door_x + 0.5 - pos_x
        let dy = door_y + 0.5 - pos_y
        if dx * dx + dy * dy < 0.8 then
            return true
        end
    end
    return false
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The menu
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The names of the choices for menu row `row` (0 the maze, 1 crystals, 2 ghosts, 3 lives,
# 4 sound)
func choice_names(row)
    if row == 0 then return MAZE_NAMES end
    if row == 1 then return CRYSTAL_NAMES end
    if row == 2 then return GHOST_NAMES end
    if row == 3 then return ["1", "3", "5"] end
    return SOUND_NAMES
end

# What menu row `row` shows for its choice, e.g. "LARGE  39 x 39" or "NORMAL  (20)"
func choice_text(row)
    let maze = choice[0]
    let name = choice_names(row)[choice[row]]
    if row == 0 then
        let size = MAZE_SIZES[maze]
        if size == 0 then size = len(MAP) end
        return name + "  " + str(size) + " x " + str(size)
    elif row == 1 then
        return name + "  (" + str(CRYSTALS_FOR[maze][choice[1]]) + ")"
    elif row == 2 then
        return name + "  (" + str(GHOSTS_FOR[maze][choice[2]]) + ")"
    elif row == 4 and not sound_card and choice[4] != SOUND_OFF then
        # without a sound card there are only the PC speaker's beeps
        return name + "  (BEEPS)"
    end
    return name
end

# The title, the choices (the one being changed in gold), and the controls
func draw_menu()
    let top = SCREEN_H / 7
    gclear(0x0B1018)
    centre(top, "MAZE 3D", GOLD, 6)
    centre(top + 80, "Pick up every crystal, then find the gold door", TEXT, 1)
    centre(top + 98, "Mind the ghosts: each one that touches you costs a life", GHOST_DOT, 1)

    # The choices, in big letters (16 pixels each): the name, then < choice >. Lined up
    # in two columns, centred as a block 30 letters wide
    let left = (SCREEN_W - 30 * 16) / 2
    for row = 0 to len(SETTING_NAMES) - 1 do
        let y = top + 135 + row * 28
        let colour = TEXT
        if row == menu_row then
            colour = GOLD
            text(left - 32, y, ">", GOLD, 2)
        end
        text(left, y, SETTING_NAMES[row], colour, 2)
        text(left + 10 * 16, y, "< " + choice_text(row) + " >", colour, 2)
    end

    # The controls in two columns: keys on the left, what they do on the right. Centring each
    # line on its own would make the columns wander, so the block is centred as a whole
    let keys = ["UP / DOWN or W / S", "LEFT / RIGHT", "MOUSE", "A / D", "SPACE / CLICK", "M", "ESC"]
    let actions = ["walk", "turn", "look around", "step sideways", "shine your light at a ghost", "show or hide the map", "back to this menu"]
    left = (SCREEN_W - 49 * 8) / 2
    for i = 0 to len(keys) - 1 do
        text(left, top + 290 + i * 18, keys[i], TEXT, 1)
        text(left + 22 * 8, top + 290 + i * 18, actions[i], DIM_TEXT, 1)
    end
    centre(top + 432, "UP / DOWN to choose, LEFT / RIGHT to change, ESC to quit", DIM_TEXT, 1)
    centre(top + 456, "ENTER OR SPACE TO START", CRYSTAL, 2)
    gshow()
end

# The menu, until Enter or Space starts a game (true) or Escape quits (false). The choices
# stay as they were for the next game.
func menu()
    let rows = len(SETTING_NAMES)
    while true do
        # the menu's music (which also starts or stops at once when SOUND is changed)
        music("menu")
        draw_menu()
        let k = key()
        let count = len(choice_names(menu_row))
        if k == KEY_ESCAPE then return false end
        if k == KEY_ENTER or k == ord(" ") then
            effect(snd_start, 80, 0)
            return true
        end
        # Up / Down and Left / Right go round: past the last is the first again
        if k == KEY_UP or k == ord("w") then
            menu_row = (menu_row + rows - 1) % rows
            effect(snd_menu_move, 60, 0)
        end
        if k == KEY_DOWN or k == ord("s") then
            menu_row = (menu_row + 1) % rows
            effect(snd_menu_move, 60, 0)
        end
        if k == KEY_LEFT or k == ord("a") then
            choice[menu_row] = (choice[menu_row] + count - 1) % count
            effect(snd_menu_change, 60, 0)
        end
        if k == KEY_RIGHT or k == ord("d") then
            choice[menu_row] = (choice[menu_row] + 1) % count
            effect(snd_menu_change, 60, 0)
        end
    end
end

# A word on the screen while a maze is made (the huge one takes a moment)
func building_screen()
    gclear(0x0B1018)
    centre(SCREEN_H / 2 - 16, "MAKING THE MAZE...", GOLD, 2)
    gshow()
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The game
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Play one game in the maze build_maze() made, from the start square to the door. Gives "won"
# for reaching the door, "caught" when the ghosts took the last life, and "menu" if Escape
# was pressed.
func play()
    pos_x = start_x
    pos_y = start_y
    # face along the first way out of the start square that isn't a wall
    let way = 0
    while way < 3 and grid[floor(start_y) + STEP_Y[way]][floor(start_x) + STEP_X[way]] != 0 do
        way = way + 1
    end
    face(way * PI / 2)
    picked = list(len(crystals), false)
    taken = 0
    lives = LIVES_FOR[choice[3]]
    reset_ghosts()
    safe_until = 0
    hurt_at = -100000
    light_ready = 0
    light_at = -100000
    stride = 0.0
    against_wall = false
    spotted_at = -100000
    music("game")
    started = time()
    # look straight ahead, and forget any mouse movement from before the game started
    look = 0
    mousemove()

    let last = time()
    let fps = 0
    let frames = 0
    let fps_since = last
    while true do
        # How long the last frame took, in seconds (at most a tenth, so one slow frame can't
        # throw you through a wall)
        let now = time()
        let seconds = smaller((now - last) / 1000.0, 0.1)
        last = now

        # Single presses: every press waits until it is read, so take them all each frame
        while haskey() do
            let k = key()
            if k == KEY_ESCAPE then return "menu" end
            if k == ord("m") or k == ord("M") then show_map = not show_map end
            if k == ord(" ") then shine_light(now) end
        end
        # A click shines the light too (holding the button shines it again as soon as it can)
        if mousedown(MOUSE_LEFT) then shine_light(now) end

        handle_movement(seconds)
        if check_pickups() then return "won" end
        work_out_steps()
        move_ghosts(seconds, now)
        if check_ghosts(now) then return "caught" end

        draw_backdrop()
        draw_walls(now)
        draw_sprites(now)
        draw_light_and_hurt(now)
        if show_map then draw_map() end
        draw_status(now, fps)
        gshow()

        # Count the frames drawn each second
        frames = frames + 1
        if now - fps_since >= 1000 then
            fps = frames
            frames = 0
            fps_since = now
        end
        frame(60)
    end
end

# The end of a game: your time, then a key for the menu. Gives false if Escape was pressed.
func finished_screen()
    let seconds = (time() - started) / 1000.0
    gclear(0x0B1018)
    centre(SCREEN_H / 3, "YOU GOT OUT", GOLD, 4)
    centre(SCREEN_H / 2, "Time: " + str(round(seconds * 10) / 10.0) + " seconds", TEXT, 2)
    centre(SCREEN_H / 2 + 60, "Press any key for the menu, ESC to quit", DIM_TEXT, 1)
    gshow()
    music("")
    effect(snd_won, 90, 880)
    return key() != KEY_ESCAPE
end

# The ghosts took your last life: a red edge, a moment to see where they got you, then how far
# you got and a key for the menu. Gives false if Escape was pressed.
func caught_screen()
    draw_hurt_edge()
    gshow()
    music("")
    effect(snd_caught, 90, 0)
    sleep(700)
    # forget the keys pressed while running away, so they don't skip this screen
    while haskey() do key() end
    gclear(0x0B1018)
    centre(SCREEN_H / 3, "CAUGHT", CAUGHT, 4)
    centre(SCREEN_H / 2, "The ghosts got you, with " + str(taken) + " of " + str(len(crystals)) + " crystals", TEXT, 2)
    centre(SCREEN_H / 2 + 60, "Press any key for the menu, ESC to quit", DIM_TEXT, 1)
    gshow()
    return key() != KEY_ESCAPE
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Start
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if not gopen() then
    print("Maze 3D needs a graphics screen.")
    exit()
end
# Draw each frame out of sight and show it whole, so nothing flickers
gbuf(true)

let SCREEN_W = gw()
let SCREEN_H = gh()
# One ray per strip: about 128 across a 1024 pixel screen
let RAYS = SCREEN_W / STRIP_WIDTH
let depths = list(RAYS, 0.0)

# Everything the functions above share. AerLang needs a variable made (with let) before a
# function can change it.
let choice = [0, 1, 1, 1, 0]   # the menu's choices: maze, crystals, ghosts, lives, sound
let menu_row = 0            # which of them Left / Right changes
let grid = []
let crystals = []
let map_picture = nil       # see make_map_picture
let picked = []
let taken = 0
let start_x = 1.5
let start_y = 1.5
let door_x = 0
let door_y = 0
let pos_x = 0.0
let pos_y = 0.0
let angle = 0.0
let dir_x = 1.0
let dir_y = 0.0
let plane_x = 0.0
let plane_y = VIEW_WIDTH
let started = 0
let show_map = true
let look = 0                # how far the horizon is moved down by looking up, in pixels
let lives = 0
let safe_until = 0          # the time until which no ghost can touch you
let hurt_at = 0             # when a ghost last touched you
let light_ready = 0         # the time from which the light can be shone again
let light_at = 0            # when it was last shone
# the ghosts (see reset_ghosts) and the step map (see work_out_steps)
let ghost_home = []
let ghost_x = []
let ghost_y = []
let ghost_to_x = []
let ghost_to_y = []
let ghost_way = []
let ghost_back = []
let ghost_chasing = []
let steps = []
let steps_x = -1            # the square the step map was worked out from
let steps_y = -1
# the sounds (see load_sounds), and what is playing
let snd_crystal = nil
let snd_door = nil
let snd_light = nil
let snd_banish = nil
let snd_hurt = nil
let snd_step = nil
let snd_bump = nil
let snd_spotted = nil
let snd_won = nil
let snd_caught = nil
let snd_menu_move = nil
let snd_menu_change = nil
let snd_start = nil
let snd_music_game = nil
let snd_music_menu = nil
let sound_card = false      # is there one? (see load_sounds)
let music_voice = -1        # splay's number for the music playing, -1 for none
let music_name = ""         # which music that is (see music)
let stride = 0.0            # squares walked since the last footstep
let against_wall = false    # walking into a wall (see footsteps)
let spotted_at = 0          # when a ghost was last heard starting to chase you

load_sounds()

# The menu, then a game, then the menu again, until Escape on the menu (or on the screen at
# the end of a game)
let playing = menu()
let result = ""
while playing do
    building_screen()
    build_maze()
    result = play()
    if result == "won" then
        playing = finished_screen()
    elif result == "caught" then
        playing = caught_screen()
    end
    if playing then playing = menu() end
end

sstop()
gclose()
print("Thanks for playing Maze 3D!")
